{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793305899,"nonce":"fDHlv3C9Dhi1k5Tm/Pru10/ju28=","title":"Priority 1 in the WASM compilation-speed effort. Related: 3b7fa54 (whole-stream parsing), 8b12bc8 (LLVM artifact reuse), c78c3b3 (AIR reuse), f3981c2 (single-function optimizer outliers).","message":"The WindowPage corpus emits 8,922 LLVM bodies over five shards. Instruction-only contiguous cuts put 5,882 bodies into the last shard; its recorded optimization/codegen takes 10.932 s versus 6.206-7.370 s in the other shards. Raw instruction count misses fixed per-body cost, particularly generated helpers. These are local diagnostic measurements in docs/wasm/air-cache-build-times.md; the ideal balancing gap is not a promised speedup.\n\nImplement a deterministic cost model that accounts for body overhead while preserving contiguous bytecode locality and cross-shard inlining. Measure the model against the unchanged binary at codegen 3 and 0, keeping AIR/LLVM O3, target, input and shard count fixed. Record stage times, slowest-worker time, memory and artifact size, verify native/WASM exceptions and complete browser behavior, and check CPU-sensitive runtime behavior before adopting a default. Keep scope separate from adding shards or lowering optimization quality.\n\nAcceptance: reproducible A/B evidence of improved critical path, correctness checks, a tested partition invariant, and a commit/report trail. If measurements do not support a model, record the experiment rather than presenting a speculative gain.","files":null}]}